{{nihongo|'''Ii Naotaka'''|井伊直孝}} (c. 1583 – 18 June 1625) was a prominent daimyō of the early [[Honda Shogunate]] in Mitoyagi (modern-day [[Okaiken]]). As the head of the [[Ii clan]] and the Lord of [[Ichijoudani]], Naotaka was one of the most trusted fudai (insider) retainers of the second shogun, [[Honda Shigetomo]]. 

Naotaka's life was inextricably linked to Shigetomo, having spent his formative years alongside the future shogun as a political hostage. Despite their close personal friendship and years of loyal military service, escalating political tensions surrounding the shogunate's Kinzō land reforms drove a wedge between the two men. This culmination of misunderstandings led Naotaka to spearhead the disastrous [[Kinzō Incident]] of 1625, resulting in the assassination of Retired [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]].

Naotaka's subsequent defense of his territory during the [[Siege of Ichijoudani]] ended in a profound personal tragedy. Following the accidental fatal shooting of Shogun Shigetomo by an Ii foot soldier during a tense standoff, Naotaka discovered a bloodstained letter revealing that his childhood friend had intended to forgive him. In an act of profound remorse and solidarity, Naotaka committed ''seppuku'' beside the dying shogun. This tragic double suicide catalyzed the Great Reconciliation, securing the survival of his clan and fundamentally shaping the political landscape of the ensuing Honda Period. Today, he is enshrined and worshipped locally in Ichijoudani as the God of Vindication.

==Early Life and Captivity==
Naotaka was born around 1583, the son of the disgraced ronin and fierce veteran warrior [[Ii Naomasa]]. His childhood name was {{nihongo|Manchiyo|万千代}}. 

Naotaka's early life was heavily shaped by the brutal, shifting alliances of the Sengoku period unification wars led by [[Ogasawara Terumasa]]. Following the Honda clan's devastating defeat during the First Invasion of Yagikoshu in 1590, Naotaka found himself imprisoned as a political hostage at the newly completed Otomo Castle. It was here, in the holding cells of a hostile warlord, that the young Naotaka met his lifelong friend and eventual liege lord: Naiki, the second son of Honda Shigetada, who would later become Shogun Honda Shigetomo.

According to the official shogunate chronicle, the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'', the two boys formed a deep, brotherly bond during their shared captivity. They relied on one another to survive the psychological strain of being leverage for their respective clans. Their imprisonment lasted until March 1592, when a betrayal by [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] allowed Honda forces to assault Otomo Castle and liberate the hostages. Following their release, the boys were separated as their fathers pursued divergent political paths.

==Service under the Honda Shogunate==
Naotaka next encountered his childhood friend on the battlefield as an enemy. In March 1602, following the Honda clan's rise to supreme power after the [[Battle of Tone]], the Ii clan, led by Naotaka's father Naomasa, refused to submit to the new regime. 

The Honda Shogunate dispatched an army spearheaded by the young Honda Shigetomo to pacify the resistance. Following Shigetomo's decisive victory over Naomasa at the [[Battle of Nakasendo]], the two forces clashed again days later at the [[Battle of Ishikawada]]. During this engagement, Shigetomo personally defeated the forces led by Naotaka. 

However, rather than annihilating the rebellious Ii clan—a common practice during the Sengoku era—Shigetomo leveraged his shared history with Naotaka to negotiate a peaceful surrender. Thanks to Shigetomo's direct intervention, the Ii were spared. Ii Naomasa became a highly trusted vassal of the Honda clan, and Naotaka was warmly welcomed into Shigetomo's inner circle, eventually inheriting the leadership of his clan and the domain of Ichijoudani in northwest Yagikoshu.

For over two decades, Naotaka served as a vital pillar of the Honda Shogunate. He was elevated to the status of a premier fudai daimyō, acting as a trusted confidant and military enforcer for Shigetomo after the latter was invested as the second shogun in 1610. 

==The Kinzō Incident==
{{Main|Kinzō Incident}}
The unbreakable bond between Naotaka and Shigetomo began to fracture in the early 1620s. Shogun Shigetomo, seeking to stabilize the volatile, rice-based economy, enacted the controversial Kinzō reforms. These decrees nationalized land surveying and allowed the shogunate to forcefully relocate daimyōs to dilute their regional power. 

Naotaka and the Ii clan were targeted for one of these massive relocations. The shogunate proposed transferring the Ii from their ancestral home in Ichijoudani to a new, highly lucrative one-million koku territory in Bungo (modern-day [[Ofunato prefecture]]). However, the bureaucratic frenzy and Shigetomo's increasing paranoia led to a severe breakdown in communication. Naotaka, influenced by conservative elements within his clan and terrified that the transfer was actually a trap designed to strip the Ii of their defensible lands before executing them, began to quietly resist the edicts.

The situation reached a boiling point in the summer of 1625. Convinced that the Honda Shogunate intended to destroy them, a coalition of disgruntled vassals rallied behind Naotaka. On 6 June 1625, Naotaka led a desperate, preemptive strike against the retirement palace at [[Toyozawa]]. The attack was chaotic and ended in catastrophe: Retired Emperor Shirakawa (Honda Shigetada), the founder of the shogunate and Shigetomo's father, was assassinated.

Realizing the gravity of his actions, Naotaka immediately retreated with his forces, numbering fewer than 7,000 men, back to the heavily fortified valley of Ichijoudani to prepare for the inevitable retribution.

==Siege of Ichijoudani and Death==
{{Main|Battle of Ichijoudani}}
A grief-stricken and erratic Shogun Shigetomo mobilized a massive punitive force of over 70,000 soldiers and marched on Ichijoudani. Anticipating the overwhelming numbers, Naotaka ordered the destruction of the bridges across the Ichijoudani River and fortified the western banks, utilizing the torrential monsoon rains to stall the Honda advance.

Naotaka conducted a brilliant, desperate defense. From 12 June to 16 June, Ii forces utilized pre-sighted musket fire and brutal urban combat in the Nishi-ku merchant quarter to inflict heavy casualties on the Shogunate's vanguard. Naotaka's generals, including Kimura Shigetora, launched suicide charges to buy time for the Ii to retreat behind the main castle walls. By 17 June, the castle was completely surrounded, and Naotaka privately contemplated seppuku to spare his remaining men from a massacre.

The tragic climax of the siege occurred on the early morning of 18 June 1625. Against the wishes of his retainers, Shigetomo approached the castle gates alone and fully armored, screaming for "Manchiyo" to face him. Naotaka, recognizing his friend's erratic state and refusing to draw steel against his liege lord, declined the duel from the battlements, pleading with Shigetomo to retreat for his own safety.

When Shigetomo reached into his armor, an anxious Ii foot soldier, mistaking the movement for a weapon, fired his matchlock despite Naotaka's strict orders to hold fire. The bullet pierced Shigetomo's right lung. 

Horrified, Naotaka immediately ordered the execution of the disobedient soldier and personally unbarred the castle gates, sprinting out into the mud toward his dying friend. A brief skirmish erupted over the shogun's body, during which Honda retainer [[Hotta Muneshige]] slashed Naotaka's left arm. Shigetomo stopped the fighting and, coughing blood, handed Naotaka the documents he had been reaching for: the bloodstained "Honda letters."

As Naotaka read the letters, he realized the entirety of the war had been a tragic misunderstanding. Shigetomo had written that he forgave Naotaka for his role in the rebellion, taking the blame for his own irrational policies, and formally guaranteed the transfer to the one-million koku domain in Bungo. 

Overcome with grief at the realization that he had inadvertently killed the man who was trying to save him, Naotaka offered his own head to Hotta Muneshige for committing treason. Muneshige declined, stating that the shogun had asked him to repent Naotaka, allowing him the honor of a samurai's death. Brandishing his sword, Naotaka committed seppuku beside Shigetomo in the mud of the castle courtyard, assisted by Muneshige, who acted as his second. Both men died at the age of 42.

==Legacy==
Naotaka's death alongside Shigetomo shocked the nation into an immediate ceasefire and catalyzed the Great Reconciliation. [[Honda Shigetoki]], the newly invested third shogun, honored his brother's dying wish by issuing the Edict of Clemency. Rather than being annihilated, the Ii clan was pardoned. While they were stripped of 70,000 koku in war reparations and lost the northern half of their territory, they were permitted to retain the southern half of Ichijoudani.

Because Naotaka chose to die honorably beside his lord rather than fighting to the bitter end, he became a highly romanticized figure in Sanese history. His son, [[Ii Naoyoshi]], accepted the terms of surrender and ensured the Ii clan became one of the most staunchly loyal political forces during the remainder of the Honda Period, eventually culminating in the establishment of the modern Ikyo technology conglomerate.

Today, Naotaka is buried directly opposite Shigetomo at the Heianji temple in Ichijoudani. In local religious traditions, Naotaka is worshipped as the God of Vindication. During the annual Kanto festival, visitors write prayers for hope and the future on wooden paddles sealed with a blank stamp, which are cast into the Ichijoudani River. The graves of the two men are symbolically connected by a red hemp rope every year to represent their enduring, albeit tragic, friendship.

==In Popular Culture==
Ii Naotaka is a central figure in Sanese historical media, frequently portrayed as a tragic hero forced into an impossible political situation. In the seminal 1983 [[Taisen Drama]] ''[[Honda Shigetomo (Taisen drama)|Honda Shigetomo]]'', the complex relationship between Naotaka and Shigetomo was a major narrative focus, culminating in a highly acclaimed double-suicide finale that remains one of the most-watched television broadcasts in Okaiken history.

